# Line-ending policy (P4-2) — this repo previously shipped with no
# .gitattributes at all, so every text file's on-disk line ending was left
# entirely to each contributor's local `core.autocrlf`. On a Windows checkout
# with `core.autocrlf=true` (the default here), git's checkout-time smudge
# filter rewrites every text blob's LF to CRLF, independent of what's stored
# in the git object. Several tests read source/fixture files from disk and
# compare their content against '\n'-joined literals or '\n'-anchored
# regexes; under CRLF those comparisons fail deterministically (see
#). Rather than pin only the files that
# happen to fail today, this repo now declares a single explicit policy so
# the outcome is deterministic on every platform and every contributor's
# config, not a race against whoever's `core.autocrlf` happens to run next:
# normalize every text file to LF in the repository, and let git re-expand
# to the platform's native ending on checkout only where that's still
# useful (it generally isn't for source/config/docs, so eol=lf is pinned
# outright rather than left to per-platform expansion).
* text=auto eol=lf

# ── Explicit binary exceptions ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# None of these currently exist as repo-tracked source files (checked via a
# repo-wide binary-extension scan on 2026-07-30 — only node_modules/ carries
# any, and that's gitignored) but are declared now so that if/when any of
# these asset types are added, git never attempts text normalization or diff
# on them, which would corrupt binary content.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.eot   binary
*.zip   binary
*.tar   binary
*.tar.gz binary
*.gz    binary

# vendored third-party assets are served with Subresource Integrity — their bytes must never be
# normalised (the bundle and stylesheet contain CR characters; text=auto eol=lf could rewrite them in a fresh clone).
src/openapi/vendor/** -text
